@page "/Home"

<PageTitle>OSDC Field</PageTitle>

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC Field</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This microservice manages Field records: their cartographic projection, WGS84 reference point, delineation and
    other typed lines, feature/membership/identity assignments, and portable versioned backups. It persists to
    SQLite, enforces optimistic concurrency on every mutation, and exposes the same operations through a REST API, an
    MCP server, and a Blazor web application, so a field created or edited in one interface is immediately visible
    through the others.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">What is a Field</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                In oil, gas, and geothermal exploitation, a <b>field</b> is the geographic and administrative area
                that groups the wells, wellbores, clusters, pipelines, and other installations sharing one reservoir,
                licence, or development project. It is a container for context, not for well trajectories: a Field
                record carries a name, a description, server-owned creation and modification timestamps, an optional
                geographic reference point, a cartographic projection, delineation lines, and the feature, membership,
                and identity assignments that describe it — while trajectory geometry, casing, and survey stations
                live in the Well and WellBore microservices, and platforms and slots live in Cluster.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Field UUIDs are caller-generated so that consuming systems can mint and reference an identifier before
                the record exists on the server, which matters when a field is planned before any well is spudded in
                it.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Cartographic projections and coordinates</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A Field can reference a <code>ProjectionDefinitionID</code> — an EarthCartographicProjection
                definition that fixes the projected coordinate reference system (datum, ellipsoid, and projection
                method) used for that field's easting/northing positions — and an optional WGS84
                <code>ReferencePoint</code> giving the field a single global location. Once a projection is assigned,
                the service performs synchronous, stateless <b>forward</b> (geographic to projected) and
                <b>inverse</b> (projected to geographic) conversions for that field, accepting input in either the
                projection datum or WGS84 and calling EarthGeodesy only when a datum transformation is actually
                required.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Requests and results are never persisted, positions are ordered batches converted atomically, angles
                are SI radians, and distances are SI metres. The same calculators are reachable directly from the
                web application's <b>Calculators</b> menu for one-off conversions outside the context of a stored
                field.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Delineation and other typed lines</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A field can carry any number of user-defined <b>delineation lines</b>: an ordered set of WGS84 points,
                an optional top and bottom depth, an optional margin distance, and a reference to a
                <code>DelineationLineTypeID</code> drawn from a local catalog. The type catalog is open-ended by
                design — the same mechanism represents a field <b>boundary</b>, a <b>licence block</b> outline, a
                <b>pipeline</b> route, or any other line a field needs to record, distinguished only by which type
                each line references.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                When a line's <code>Margin</code> is set, the service also computes offset
                <b>boundary lines</b> — interior and/or exterior, closed or open depending on the input geometry —
                and stores them as read-only <code>CalculatedBoundaryLines</code> alongside the original points. Lines
                are drawn and edited on the <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/Field">Field</MudLink> page, and their types
                are managed on <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldDelineationLineTypes">Delineation Line Types</MudLink>.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Features, memberships, and identities</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Every field can carry any number of <b>feature</b> assignments (arbitrary field characteristics),
                <b>membership</b> assignments (placing the field in a basin, licence, or operator grouping), and
                <b>identity</b> assignments (symbolic references such as a licence number or an operator field name),
                each drawn from a locally managed catalog of categories and options. A category can be marked
                exclusive, so only one option from it applies at a time, and optionally time-bounded, so an
                assignment carries a validity period.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                The catalogs are managed on <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldFeatures">Field Features</MudLink>,
                <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldMemberships">Field Memberships</MudLink>, and
                <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldIdentities">Field Identities</MudLink> — a category, option, or
                identity definition cannot be deleted while a field still references it, and removing an option from
                a category is likewise rejected while any field assignment still uses it.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Conventions this microservice follows</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Field follows the same conventions as the other OSDC drilling microservices, so behavior learned on one
        transfers directly to the others. Resource identifiers are caller-generated UUIDs rather than server
        sequence numbers. Every mutation is checked with <b>optimistic concurrency</b>: an update or delete must echo
        back the <code>LastModificationDate</code> (as <code>expectedModifiedUtc</code>) from the caller's latest
        read, and a stale request is rejected with HTTP 409 rather than silently overwriting a concurrent change.
        Updates are full replacements — a PUT must include everything that should remain stored, not just the
        changed fields.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Field-owned catalogs — features, memberships, identities, and delineation line types — are validated on every
        write: a field cannot reference a category, option, or definition that does not exist, and none of those
        catalog entries can be deleted, nor can an option be removed from a category, while any field still
        references it. Conflicts are reported with the affected Field UUIDs rather than a generic failure.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Backup and restore</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Backups are logical, portable JSON, not a raw database copy. From the
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldBackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink> page, export <b>all</b> fields
        or an ordered <b>selection</b> into one versioned document that carries the complete fields together with
        only the feature, membership, identity, and delineation-line-type catalog entries they actually reference —
        enough to reproduce the fields on another installation without pulling in unrelated catalog clutter.
        Referenced projection definitions travel as external UUIDs and are not resolved during export.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Restoring the same document back offers a <b>conflict policy</b> — fail if any field UUID already exists
        (<code>FailIfExists</code>), or replace matching fields while creating the rest (<code>ReplaceExisting</code>)
        — and a <b>catalog policy</b> — map onto existing local catalog entries by UUID or normalized name
        (<code>MapExisting</code>), or additionally create the missing ones locally with server-generated UUIDs
        (<code>MapOrCreateMissing</code>). Format, version, timestamps, and referenced UUIDs are validated before
        writing, and catalog mapping, catalog creation, and every field write happen inside one SQLite transaction,
        so a restore of many fields either commits completely or leaves the database untouched.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Using this web application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Open <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/Field">Field</MudLink> to browse, search, create, edit, and remove fields,
        draw and edit their delineation lines, and manage their feature, membership, and identity assignments. Manage
        the shared catalogs on <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldFeatures">Field Features</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldMemberships">Field Memberships</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldIdentities">Field Identities</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldDelineationLineTypes">Delineation Line Types</MudLink>, and move data
        between installations on <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldBackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink>.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The <b>Survey Display</b> menu shows <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldTrajectories">Field Trajectories</MudLink>
        and <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldSurveyRuns">Field Survey Runs</MudLink> with the field's delineation
        lines overlaid, so trajectories and survey runs can be reviewed in the context of the field boundary and any
        other typed lines. The <b>Contextual Data</b> menu gives read access to
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/Cluster">Clusters</MudLink> and the
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/ProjectionDefinition">Cartographic Projection</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/GeodeticDatum">Geodetic Datum</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/Spheroid">Spheroid</MudLink> definitions a field's coordinate conversions rely
        on, each backed by its own microservice and shown here through reusable Razor components.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <b>Calculators</b> menu exposes the same synchronous, stateless evaluations used elsewhere in OSDC —
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/FieldCartographicConverter">Cartographic Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/EarthVerticalDatumCalculation">Vertical Datum Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/EarthGravityCalculation">Earth Gravity Evaluation</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/EarthMagneticFieldCalculation">Earth Magnetic Field Evaluation</MudLink> — for
        one-off conversions and lookups while working a field, and
        <MudLink Href="/Field/webapp/StatisticsField">Usage Statistics</MudLink> reports per-endpoint request counts
        for this service replica. All numeric fields follow the unit system selected for the page through the OSDC
        unit-reference system.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same field data is reachable through several interfaces built on the same underlying store, so it can be
        managed interactively, scripted, embedded in other Blazor applications, or wired into AI agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for browsing, editing, backing up, and
                    restoring fields, drawing delineation lines, inspecting contextual data, running calculators, and
                    reviewing usage statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/Field/api</code> for field CRUD, delineation-line-type, feature,
                    membership, and identity catalog management, coordinate conversion, batch export/restore, and
                    usage statistics, documented and explorable through the Swagger UI at
                    <code>/Field/api/swagger</code>. Suited to scripts, backend integration, and any HTTP client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at <code>/Field/api/mcp</code> (and
                    WebSocket at <code>/Field/api/mcp/ws</code>), publishing 41 tools — every non-statistics REST
                    operation plus connectivity <code>ping</code> — with strict, closed input/output JSON Schemas, so
                    AI agents and MCP-aware tooling can query, create, update, convert coordinates for, and back up
                    fields directly. Coordinate-conversion and batch-restore tools are marked accordingly, and usage
                    statistics stay REST-only.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the Field, catalog, delineation editing, backup/restore, and
                    survey-display pages are published as the <code>OSDC.Drilling.Field.WebPages</code> NuGet
                    package, so other Blazor applications — such as the Well and WellBore web apps — can embed the
                    same field context and unit-aware controls instead of re-implementing this UI.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
